WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.284 --> 00:00:01.896
(upbeat salsa dance music)

00:00:01.896 --> 00:00:04.710
(mysterious whispering)

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:08.518
- I have a slightly complicated
relationship with breakfast.

00:00:08.518 --> 00:00:10.200
(upbeat music)

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.101
I (censor beep) hate American breakfast.

00:00:12.101 --> 00:00:15.136
(the word "Breakfast" echoing)

00:00:15.136 --> 00:00:16.563
So, the first meal of the day.

00:00:16.563 --> 00:00:18.229
- Breakfast.
- Breakfast.

00:00:18.229 --> 00:00:19.062
- Breakfast.
- Breakfast.

00:00:19.062 --> 00:00:21.004
- Break-fast!!

00:00:21.004 --> 00:00:22.800
- A couple of a years
ago I made several videos

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:25.140
condemning American breakfast
for being too sweet.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:27.930
And then proceeded to tell
you about how cultured I was,

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:30.030
because I discovered other
breakfast traditions,

00:00:30.030 --> 00:00:31.470
and integrated them into my life

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:33.240
in order to stop hating breakfast.

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:34.230
And it worked.

00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:36.420
But, one thing I failed to mention during

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:37.890
all of this breakfast commentary,

00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:41.561
is that I eat Doritos for breakfast.

00:00:41.561 --> 00:00:43.898
(music slo-mo to halting)
Like all the time.

00:00:43.898 --> 00:00:44.731
(lively music)

00:00:44.731 --> 00:00:45.564
Doritos.

00:00:45.564 --> 00:00:47.534
(multiple voices chanting "Doritos.")

00:00:47.534 --> 00:00:52.534
(Doritos crunching)
(lively music)

00:00:53.355 --> 00:00:58.355
(lively music)
(Doritos crunching)

00:00:58.918 --> 00:01:02.550
(lively music)
(Doritos crunching)

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:05.790
Yes, I eat them with
chopsticks for obvious reasons.

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:09.018
See my fingers? Squeaky clean.

00:01:09.018 --> 00:01:11.601
(lively music)

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.750
Wait! I gotta tell you
something really quick.

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.970
I made a video about China
and the United States

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:20.490
and their economic war over microchips.

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:21.780
It's really insane.

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:23.670
That video is live right now.

00:01:23.670 --> 00:01:25.080
You can go watch it on Nebula.

00:01:25.080 --> 00:01:27.690
In fact, you can watch every
one of my videos a week early.

00:01:27.690 --> 00:01:30.300
And it costs you just over
one U.S. dollar a month,

00:01:30.300 --> 00:01:32.970
using a hack that I will
explain at the end of the video.

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:37.000
For now, let's just keep
swimming in a sea of Doritos.

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:42.000
(lively music)
(echoing voices)

00:01:43.405 --> 00:01:45.480
(lively music slows down)

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.430
but no, I'm serious. This is a real thing.

00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:49.560
One of Nick's jobs, our studio manager,

00:01:49.560 --> 00:01:51.930
is to make sure that there
are Doritos always stocked

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:53.070
in the studio.

00:01:53.070 --> 00:01:55.070
And to be honest, he
does an excellent job.

00:01:55.070 --> 00:01:56.793
- [Interviewer] Do you like you job, Nick?

00:01:56.793 --> 00:01:57.743
- I love my job.

00:01:57.743 --> 00:01:58.778
- [Interviewer] You better
(censor beep) your job, dude.

00:01:58.778 --> 00:01:59.625
- Yeah.

00:01:59.625 --> 00:02:00.660
I work every day. Psych!

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:03.000
- Nick has another part of his
job that's slightly related,

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:04.320
I'm now realizing.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:05.370
I'll tell you about that later.

00:02:05.370 --> 00:02:07.110
I was recently at a wedding in Canada,

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:08.340
getting slightly rowdy.

00:02:08.340 --> 00:02:10.770
And during the party, I had a conversation

00:02:10.770 --> 00:02:11.880
with my friend, Becky.

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:13.740
I was telling her about
how much I love Doritos.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:15.270
And especially for breakfast.

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:17.190
The first thing Becky did
was tell me how much better

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:20.430
Canadian chips are,
especially Canadian Doritos.

00:02:20.430 --> 00:02:23.223
She promised that she would
send me Canadian Doritos.

00:02:24.450 --> 00:02:25.693
And indeed, she did.

00:02:25.693 --> 00:02:27.120
(wrappings crackling)
Oh, yes! (chuckling)

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:30.120
We have Doritos from Canada,
and we have chopsticks.

00:02:30.120 --> 00:02:30.990
Thank you, Becky.

00:02:30.990 --> 00:02:33.870
But then she also asked
me why the breakfast snob,

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:35.250
over here who's like condemning

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:36.780
all things American breakfast,

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:39.360
eats Doritos for the
first meal of the day?

00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:41.370
But it turns out there's
an answer to this.

00:02:41.370 --> 00:02:43.110
One that required a
little bit of research,

00:02:43.110 --> 00:02:44.220
some expert interviews,

00:02:44.220 --> 00:02:46.140
an a conversation with the internet's best

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:47.310
food science explainer.

00:02:47.310 --> 00:02:48.930
- And this is just me spit-balling.

00:02:48.930 --> 00:02:51.210
And I'm not a scientist, I
just play one on the internet.

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:53.430
- The answer to this is yet another stop

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:55.500
on my long, strange journey

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.090
of trying to understand breakfast.

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:59.100
And its connection to human evolution,

00:02:59.100 --> 00:03:00.420
and ancient traditions.

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:03.990
And perhaps most
importantly, today's economy.

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:06.570
Where we live in a world
where literal scientists

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:08.550
sit around and engineer food,

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:10.350
so that we can't get enough of it.

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:12.524
- [Announcer] Try Doritos.
(man crunching)

00:03:12.524 --> 00:03:13.770
They taste as good as they crunch.

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:15.090
- He's in a better place now.

00:03:15.090 --> 00:03:17.310
- It's corn, tomato, onions, and spices.

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:18.143
- Doritos!

00:03:19.363 --> 00:03:21.743
(Dorito dust blowing)

00:03:21.743 --> 00:03:26.261
(upbeat music)
(man humming)

00:03:26.261 --> 00:03:27.843
- Oh my God, look at this.

00:03:27.843 --> 00:03:29.755
(upbeat music)

00:03:29.755 --> 00:03:31.830
(items in box crackling)
Oh-ho, yes!

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:33.900
Dear Johnny, hope you like cheese.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:35.583
P.S., congrats again on the Emmy.

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:37.848
Thanks, Becky.

00:03:37.848 --> 00:03:38.681
(bag crinkling)

00:03:38.681 --> 00:03:41.313
Today we got Zesty Cheese.

00:03:42.825 --> 00:03:46.742
(speaking in foreign language)

00:03:48.124 --> 00:03:49.470
(bag tears open)

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:50.403
Zesty cheese.

00:03:52.287 --> 00:03:53.130
(crunching Doritos)

00:03:53.130 --> 00:03:56.100
Oh my God! It's like
nacho cheese with a kick.

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:57.266
Oh, yeah.

00:03:57.266 --> 00:03:58.116
This is good.

00:03:58.116 --> 00:03:58.949
(crunching Doritos)

00:03:58.949 --> 00:04:00.000
Dude, they're extra salty, too.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:02.430
- The cornerstone of any
nutritious breakfast.

00:04:02.430 --> 00:04:03.570
- This is amazing.

00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:05.610
Canada kills it with chips.

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:07.890
Okay, let's get this started.

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:10.430
I think first we need
to talk about Doritos.

00:04:10.430 --> 00:04:11.970
(upbeat music)

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:12.870
- [Announcer] Cool Ranch flavored

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:14.520
Doritos brand tortilla chips.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:16.285
One heck of a crunch.

00:04:16.285 --> 00:04:18.240
(bag crinkling)

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.603
- This is a Dorito.

00:04:20.603 --> 00:04:24.450
It is a triangle of mashed up
corn, that is baked, fried,

00:04:24.450 --> 00:04:27.505
and coated with a magical cheesy powder.

00:04:27.505 --> 00:04:28.860
(blowing on dust)

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:33.000
This orange dust is a mix
of garlic, Romano cheese,

00:04:33.000 --> 00:04:34.380
and cheddar cheese.

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:36.900
We got a total of 25
different ingredients on here.

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:38.580
And in the case of Cool Ranch-

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:40.503
- No other chip tastes like Cool Ranch.

00:04:40.503 --> 00:04:41.357
(bag crinkles)

00:04:41.357 --> 00:04:42.190
- Basically the same ingredients.

00:04:42.190 --> 00:04:43.740
Like Cool Ranch has a couple of things

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:44.940
that I can't really pronoun here.

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:47.130
Like Sodium Acetate,

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:50.746
Disodium Inosinate, Inosinate.

00:04:50.746 --> 00:04:52.329
Disodium Guanylate.

00:04:53.605 --> 00:04:54.510
Guanylate.

00:04:54.510 --> 00:04:55.470
And crucially,

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:56.790
both of these bags,

00:04:56.790 --> 00:05:00.930
plus Becky's Canadian flavor,
contain a magic powder

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:02.910
called Monosodium Glutamate.

00:05:02.910 --> 00:05:05.310
A mysterious thing that I think

00:05:05.310 --> 00:05:07.350
is at the center of why
I love this so much.

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:09.003
And why I want it for breakfast.

00:05:09.901 --> 00:05:10.996
- My precious.

00:05:10.996 --> 00:05:15.780
(snapshots flashing)
(lively music)

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:17.867
- This is really delicious.

00:05:17.867 --> 00:05:20.280
(lively music)
(crowd &amp; Mrs. Biden giggling)

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:22.530
- This magic ingredient
was invented in Japan

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:23.730
a hundred or so years ago.

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:26.070
When this Japanese chemist
was supposedly trying

00:05:26.070 --> 00:05:29.310
to figure out why his wife's
soup tastes so damn good.

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:30.210
He did a bunch of science,

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:32.850
and realized it was this strip of kelp,

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:34.350
which is basically like seaweed,

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:36.390
but technically it's
not seaweed, it's kelp.

00:05:36.390 --> 00:05:37.560
This was the key ingredient.

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:40.380
He saw his wife put this
strip of kelp in boiling water

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:41.940
at the beginning of soup-making.

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:43.020
And then taking it out.

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:44.850
And whatever happened
during that short time

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:46.290
made all the difference.

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:47.123
Wait a minute.

00:05:47.123 --> 00:05:49.961
This same thing happens
every Monday in my studio.

00:05:49.961 --> 00:05:50.910
(upbeat music)

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:53.460
Oh, it smells good in here,
Nick, what you making?

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:55.530
- Miso soup for miso Monday.

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:56.520
- Miso Monday.

00:05:56.520 --> 00:05:59.040
Every Monday we have
Miso soup in this office.

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:00.690
Because like Doritos,

00:06:00.690 --> 00:06:03.630
Miso is a very important
part of my morning.

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:04.920
- Yeah, makes the whole week good.

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:06.260
It really does.
- [Nick] A big pot of miso.

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:08.730
So now with Japanese kelp.

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:09.647
From (speaking in foreign language).

00:06:10.682 --> 00:06:11.515
I bought it at my local Japanese market,

00:06:11.515 --> 00:06:13.230
Hanna Japanese Market.

00:06:13.230 --> 00:06:14.520
Love you guys.

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:15.720
- [Host] And what do you do with it?

00:06:15.720 --> 00:06:17.370
- [Nick] Put it in there
like while it's hot,

00:06:17.370 --> 00:06:19.730
growing boil, for about 20 minutes.

00:06:19.730 --> 00:06:20.563
(quick musical interlude)

00:06:20.563 --> 00:06:22.710
Take it out, adds that
essential sea flavor.

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:24.570
We got the spigot on the o there side.

00:06:24.570 --> 00:06:27.810
So when the soup's done,
we're gonna dispense it.

00:06:27.810 --> 00:06:28.643
Ow, it's very hot.

00:06:28.643 --> 00:06:30.660
- We will be back for the miso.

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:32.510
Thanks, Nick.
- [Nick] See you guys.

00:06:34.241 --> 00:06:35.074
(record scratching)
- Wait!

00:06:35.074 --> 00:06:37.735
But what does this have
to do with Doritos?

00:06:37.735 --> 00:06:38.568
(record scratching)

00:06:38.568 --> 00:06:39.450
- Everything.

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:41.430
This has everything to do with Doritos.

00:06:41.430 --> 00:06:43.560
Let me explain.
(serious violin music)

00:06:43.560 --> 00:06:45.442
- Hm, I really want some miso soup.

00:06:45.442 --> 00:06:47.070
(quiet explosion and tiny bell ringing)

00:06:47.070 --> 00:06:49.290
Oh my God! Miso soup!

00:06:49.290 --> 00:06:50.910
- Everything is.

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:53.040
Because this Japanese chemist discovered

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:54.750
that this seaweed, or kelp,

00:06:54.750 --> 00:06:57.150
contained a bunch of one special molecule

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:59.190
called glutamate, or glutamic acid.

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:00.720
It's actually an amino acid.

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.700
And, it's actually the
most abundant amino acid

00:07:02.700 --> 00:07:04.320
that you find in breast milk.

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:05.400
Like the first thing you taste
(baby cooing)

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:06.360
when you come into this world.
(baby cooing)

00:07:06.360 --> 00:07:07.920
When glutamate is present in your mouth,

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:11.010
it makes everything you're
eating taste more delicious,

00:07:11.010 --> 00:07:12.540
and more substantial.

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:14.250
Meaty and more savory.

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:15.450
The guy kept poking around,

00:07:15.450 --> 00:07:18.270
and discovered that this
delicious chemical reaction

00:07:18.270 --> 00:07:20.190
was not just happening in his wife's soup

00:07:20.190 --> 00:07:21.780
from the kelp she was putting in,

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:23.490
but also in a bunch of
other delicious foods.

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:24.990
Like ripe tomatoes,

00:07:24.990 --> 00:07:26.790
meat and cheese.

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:29.310
Broths, mushrooms, asparagus,

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:31.380
fish sauce, parmesan cheese.

00:07:31.380 --> 00:07:33.390
The Japanese chemist
claimed that glutamate

00:07:33.390 --> 00:07:35.670
had such a significant impact on taste,

00:07:35.670 --> 00:07:37.950
that it deserved its own classification.

00:07:37.950 --> 00:07:38.783
At the time,

00:07:38.783 --> 00:07:40.500
there were four major classes of taste.

00:07:40.500 --> 00:07:42.450
Sweet, sour, salty, and bitter.

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:43.800
He said there should be a fifth.

00:07:43.800 --> 00:07:44.700
Oh and by the way,

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:46.890
if you've ever had this
taste map in school,

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:47.723
like I did,

00:07:47.723 --> 00:07:49.830
of where your taste buds taste things.

00:07:49.830 --> 00:07:50.970
That's kind of BS.

00:07:50.970 --> 00:07:52.620
Your taste receptors overlap,

00:07:52.620 --> 00:07:54.210
and they're sort of all
over on your tongue.

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:55.950
Anyway, that was a kind
of annoying side note.

00:07:55.950 --> 00:07:56.783
Let's get going.

00:07:56.783 --> 00:07:58.350
He called this taste "Umami."

00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:00.240
Which beautiful in Japanese,

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:03.327
roughly translates to "Deliciousness."

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:06.330
Why call it some technical thing,

00:08:06.330 --> 00:08:07.477
when you'd just be like,

00:08:07.477 --> 00:08:09.000
"It's deliciousness."

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:10.590
That is what it'll be classified.

00:08:10.590 --> 00:08:12.420
Umami, the taste experience,

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:15.270
was officially recognized
by scientists in the 90s.

00:08:15.270 --> 00:08:17.250
And there's been a lot of
really amazing work on it.

00:08:17.250 --> 00:08:18.750
But story is really just beginning.

00:08:18.750 --> 00:08:20.730
Because this Japanese chemist found a way

00:08:20.730 --> 00:08:24.120
to extract this delicious
flavor from the kelp,

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:27.240
and to mass produce it in the
form of a flakey white salt.

00:08:27.240 --> 00:08:29.580
He called it monosodium glutamate,

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:31.710
or M-S-G.

00:08:31.710 --> 00:08:34.200
Pure glutamate put into
the form of a salt.

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:39.060
And this is the key ingredient
that coats all of our Doritos

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:41.394
and makes them delicious.

00:08:41.394 --> 00:08:43.050
(Dorito crunching)

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:45.030
And this is where it all comes together.

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:48.900
Linking my Dorito craving
to my love for miso soup.

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:51.840
One, a wholesome soup with
countless benefits to my health.

00:08:51.840 --> 00:08:54.630
And the other, a strip of fried corn paste

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:56.310
with really no nutritional value.

00:08:56.310 --> 00:08:58.260
And yet, I crave both
of them when I show up

00:08:58.260 --> 00:08:59.220
to work in the morning.

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:01.110
Because what I'm really craving here,

00:09:01.110 --> 00:09:03.420
is the chemical reaction
that produces umami

00:09:03.420 --> 00:09:05.190
in a savory deliciousness.

00:09:05.190 --> 00:09:07.950
Either from the glutamic
acid in Nick's kelp,

00:09:07.950 --> 00:09:09.630
or from the special white powder

00:09:09.630 --> 00:09:10.830
that I often sniff up.

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:14.343
I mean that coats my, the
surface of the Doritos.

00:09:16.283 --> 00:09:19.581
(bag crinkling)
(Doritos crunching)

00:09:19.581 --> 00:09:22.164
(upbeat music)

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:25.367
I think it's ready. C'mon.

00:09:29.367 --> 00:09:31.284
It's ready! Here we go!

00:09:33.131 --> 00:09:33.964
Mm.

00:09:34.964 --> 00:09:36.259
Okay.

00:09:36.259 --> 00:09:37.380
Don't mind if I do.

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:40.513
Hey Nick, by the way, is MSG bad for you?

00:09:40.513 --> 00:09:41.854
- I don't know.

00:09:41.854 --> 00:09:43.290
Why don't you ask Adam Ragusea?

00:09:43.290 --> 00:09:44.643
- Yeah, Adam Ragusea.

00:09:45.674 --> 00:09:47.507
- [Nick] I don't know.

00:09:48.523 --> 00:09:49.356
- Hey, Adam!

00:09:49.356 --> 00:09:50.190
Oh, there we go.

00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:51.360
Hi!
- [Adam] What's up?

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:53.160
- Oh, maybe I'm not mic'd.

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:53.993
Oh, hold on.

